---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Mark from "../components/Mark.astro";
import { byLicence, foundryMitDate } from "../data/open-source";

const mitProjects = byLicence("mit");
const tiers = [
  {
    key: "mit",
    label: "MIT",
    count: mitProjects.length,
    can: "Use it, change it, sell what you build with it.",
    names: mitProjects.map((project) => (project.state === "paused" ? `${project.name} · paused` : project.name)),
  },
  {
    key: "source-available",
    label: "Source-available",
    count: byLicence("source-available").length,
    can: `Foundry's app. Free to use at work, not to resell. MIT on ${foundryMitDate}.`,
    names: byLicence("source-available").map((project) => project.name),
  },
  {
    key: "closed",
    label: "Closed",
    count: byLicence("closed").length,
    can: "Kingdom runs as a hosted service. Oracle is not published.",
    names: byLicence("closed").map((project) => project.name),
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="inixiative: technology for cooperation"
  description="Aron Greenspan's open building blocks for SaaS apps, a workspace that gets coding agents to use what your codebase already has, and designs for cooperation."
  domain="inixiative"
>
  <script slot="head" is:inline>
    // Every primitive's GitHub homepage, and posts from before the rebuild, link to /#<repo>.
    // Those sections now live on their own pages; send the old anchors there.
    (function () {
      var id = location.hash.slice(1);
      if (!id) return;
      var blocks = ["primitives", "template", "json-rules", "permissions", "transitions", "prisma-map", "atlas", "rules-builder", "config", "gloss", "conditional-form"];
      var agentic = ["foundry", "agent-session", "archive", "signet", "kcp", "kingdom", "oracle", "bench", "hivemind"];
      var target = null;
      if (id === "think" || id === "governance") target = "/governance/";
      else if (id === "agentic") target = "/agentic/";
      else if (id === "building-blocks") target = "/building-blocks/";
      else if (id === "foundry-oracle") target = "/agentic/#oracle";
      else if (blocks.indexOf(id) !== -1) target = "/building-blocks/#" + id;
      else if (agentic.indexOf(id) !== -1) target = "/agentic/#" + id;
      if (target) location.replace(target);
    })();
  </script>

  <section class="hero">
    <span class="hero-mark"><Mark /></span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Technology for cooperation.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I'm Aron Greenspan. inixiative is a decade of lessons from building software, packaged so I
      don't have to figure the same things out on every project, and so you can start from them too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="building-blocks" aria-labelledby="building-blocks-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Building blocks</span>
      <h2 id="building-blocks-title">Stop rediscovering what you need to build.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Across my career I kept running into the same shapes of problem. More often than not, people
        built a one-off solution for each, often right next to another problem with the same shape. I
        pulled those problems out and built the best-shaped solution I could for each, so it can be
        reused wherever that problem shows up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Template is where they come together. It is like shadcn, but for the whole SaaS app: you start
        a product from it and own the code. On day one, people can sign in and work together in teams,
        each person sees only what they're allowed to, sensitive data stays encrypted, screens update
        live, and the changes you choose to track are kept in a log with who made them.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the problems almost every product has, such as deciding who can do what, I built pieces you
        can use without the template: a rules engine, a permissions library, the screens for building
        rules, and a way to say which step a record can move to next. Most of them run in production at
        the company where I work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/building-blocks/">The building blocks</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" aria-labelledby="developer-experience-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Developer experience</span>
      <h2 id="developer-experience-title">Tools for working on the code.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        These tools help me build, and none of them ends up in your product. Atlas keeps a map of what
        is where in the code. Gloss keeps notes about the code out of the code, and hands them to an
        agent when it reads that file. Config gives every project the same setup.
      </p>
      <p>
        Template comes with its own: several copies of a project can run side by side without colliding,
        a guided setup creates a new project's accounts and services, and automatic checks stop code that
        rebuilds something the project already has.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/building-blocks/#developer-experience">Developer experience</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="agentic" aria-labelledby="agentic-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Agentic</span>
      <h2 id="agentic-title">An agent that works like you do.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Then I started building with coding agents, and they ignored the answers. In a project that
        already had a sign-in system, an agent built a second one beside it. I kept correcting the same
        kind of thing: agents rewrote small tools the project already had. If agents used the template as
        written, I wouldn't have needed to build Foundry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stop repeating yourself to Claude.</strong> Foundry is the workspace around your agent,
        on the Claude Code and Codex subscriptions you already pay for. Small expert agents called
        Wardens notice what your request touches and point your agent at what your project already has,
        and where it's written down. Your agent decides what to use. Giving your work a voice.
      </p>
      <p>I built the rest of the agentic tools for the same problem:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Kingdom holds your connected accounts and apps, and lets you decide who and what can reach them.</li>
        <li>Archive keeps a copy of every conversation with your agents.</li>
        <li>Signet is a pass to use one of your connected accounts, which you can take back.</li>
        <li>KCP will let your command-line tools use those accounts without keeping the keys on your computer. <span class="tag" data-tone="progress">In progress</span></li>
        <li>Oracle checks whether a correction you gave an agent stuck (closed source).</li>
        <li>Artifactory will let you share and discuss what your agents make, with access from Kingdom. <span class="tag" data-tone="progress">Planned</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/foundry/">How Foundry works</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/agentic/">All the agentic tools</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" data-domain="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Governance</span>
      <h2 id="governance-title">Designs for cooperation at scale.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        The long-term goal is tools for people, and their agents, to cooperate. The whitepaper and the
        proposals describe how groups set rules, hold each other accountable and invest together without
        a central referee.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first piece I plan to build there is the Inixiative project: a group pools money and releases
        it only when its members agree a milestone was met, starting with cattle trusts in Uruguay and
        Argentina. <span class="tag" data-tone="progress">Planned</span>
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/governance/">The proposals</a>
      <a class="text-link" href="/governance/#planned">The Inixiative project</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section story" aria-labelledby="open-source-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Open source</span>
      <h2 id="open-source-title">Committed to open source.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Almost everything we build is MIT. Here's how the rest splits.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="licence-tiers">
      {tiers.map((tier) => (
        <li class="licence-tier" data-licence={tier.key}>
          <div class="licence-head">
            <span class="licence-count">{tier.count}</span>
            <span class="licence-label">{tier.label}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="licence-can">{tier.can}</p>
          <ul class="licence-names">
            {tier.names.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/open-source/">The full table</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
